  <!-- EDUCATIONAL CONTENT SLOT -->
  <Fragment slot="content">
    <section class="disc-content">
      
      <!-- 1. HOW TO USE THE CALCULATOR -->
      <h2>How to Use the Discount & Sale Price Calculator</h2>
      <p>
        Finding accurate discount amounts, percentage savings, or pre-discount retail values is simple with our multi-mode tool:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Select the type of discount calculation:</strong> Choose between <em>Find Sale Price</em> (standard % or fixed discount), <em>Find Discount %</em> (reverse rate from sale price), <em>Find Original Price</em> (restore pre-discount value), or <em>Multiple Discounts</em> (stacked promotional codes).</li>
        <li><strong>Enter the original price or sale price:</strong> Type the retail amount in your local currency.</li>
        <li><strong>Enter the discount percentage or fixed discount amount:</strong> Specify the promotional percentage (e.g. 20%) or cash markdown ($50).</li>
        <li><strong>Add additional discounts if needed:</strong> In multiple discount mode, enter secondary and tertiary coupon rates.</li>
        <li><strong>Enable sales tax if you want to estimate the final price after tax:</strong> Toggle the tax option and input your local tax percentage.</li>
        <li><strong>Click Calculate Discount:</strong> Review the itemized savings, checkout total, and mathematical breakdown.</li>
        <li><strong>Download the result as a PDF:</strong> Save a branded PDF report for your shopping budget or retail invoices.</li>
      </ol>

      <!-- 2. HOW TO CALCULATE A DISCOUNT -->
      <h2>How to Calculate a Discount</h2>
      <p>
        Calculating a standard percentage discount involves two simple arithmetic steps:
      </p>
      <p>
        <code>Discount Amount = Original Price × (Discount Percentage ÷ 100)</code><br />
        <code>Sale Price = Original Price − Discount Amount</code>
      </p>
      <p>
        For instance, if an item originally costs <strong>$500.00</strong> with a <strong>20% discount</strong>:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><code>Discount Amount = $500 × 0.20 = $100.00 Saved</code></li>
        <li><code>Final Sale Price = $500 − $100 = $400.00</code></li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-xs text-slate-500 italic">
        * This example is for illustration. Actual point-of-sale totals may vary depending on local sales tax and merchant checkout rounding rules.
      </p>

      <!-- 3. HOW TO CALCULATE DISCOUNT PERCENTAGE -->
      <h2>How to Calculate Discount Percentage</h2>
      <p>
        When you know the original tag price and the discounted price on a receipt, you can calculate the exact percentage saved using:
      </p>
      <p>
        <code>Discount Percentage = ((Original Price − Sale Price) ÷ Original Price) × 100</code>
      </p>
      <p>
        For example, if a jacket marked down from $120.00 is sold for $90.00:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><code>Discount Amount = $120 − $90 = $30.00</code></li>
        <li><code>Discount % = ($30 ÷ $120) × 100 = 25% Off</code></li>
      </ul>

      <!-- 4. HOW MULTIPLE DISCOUNTS WORK -->
      <h2>How Multiple Discounts Work</h2>
      <p>
        A frequent misconception in retail shopping is that stacked discounts add together linearly. For example, a <strong>20% storewide sale</strong> combined with an <strong>extra 10% coupon code</strong> does not equal a 30% discount.
      </p>
      <p>
        Sequential discounts are calculated compounding off the already reduced subtotal:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Original Price:</strong> $1,000.00</li>
        <li><strong>After 20% Store Discount:</strong> $1,000 − $200 = $800.00</li>
        <li><strong>After Extra 10% Coupon:</strong> $800 − (10% of $800) = $800 − $80 = <strong>$720.00</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Total Money Saved:</strong> $280.00</li>
        <li><strong>Effective Overall Discount:</strong> <code>($280 ÷ $1,000) × 100 = 28.0%</code> (not 30.0%)</li>
      </ul>

      <!-- 5. DISCOUNT VS SALE PRICE -->
      <h2>Discount vs Sale Price</h2>
      <p>
        Understanding core retail pricing terms ensures accurate budgeting:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Original Price (MSRP / List Price):</strong> The baseline retail sticker price before any promotional reductions.</li>
        <li><strong>Discount Amount:</strong> The monetary value deducted from the original price (what you save).</li>
        <li><strong>Discount Percentage:</strong> The markdown rate expressed as a percentage of the original price.</li>
        <li><strong>Sale Price:</strong> The reduced price before applicable taxes and post-sale fees.</li>
        <li><strong>Final Price Including Tax:</strong> The actual out-of-pocket checkout total after applying statutory sales taxes.</li>
      </ul>

      <!-- 6. SALES TAX AFTER DISCOUNT -->
      <h2>Is Sales Tax Calculated Before or After a Discount?</h2>
      <p>
        In most US states and international retail jurisdictions, statutory sales tax is calculated on the <strong>net discounted sale price</strong> rather than the original manufacturer list price.
      </p>
      <p>
        However, certain exceptions apply:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Store Discounts & Instant Coupons:</strong> Tax is assessed on the lower sale price paid by the customer.</li>
        <li><strong>Manufacturer Coupons / Rebates:</strong> In some jurisdictions, because the manufacturer reimburses the retailer for the coupon value, sales tax is assessed on the pre-coupon full price.</li>
      </ul>

      <!-- 7. PRACTICAL EXAMPLES -->
      <h2>Discount & Sale Price Examples</h2>

      <h3>Example 1: Percentage Discount</h3>
      <p>
        A flat-screen TV listed at $800.00 is offered at 15% off during a holiday weekend. Discount amount is $800 × 0.15 = $120.00, resulting in a sale price of <strong>$680.00</strong>.
      </p>

      <h3>Example 2: Fixed Amount Discount</h3>
      <p>
        A smartphone retailing for $650.00 has an instant cash markdown coupon of $75.00. The sale price is $650 − $75 = <strong>$575.00</strong> (representing an effective 11.54% savings).
      </p>

      <h3>Example 3: Multiple Sequential Discounts</h3>
      <p>
        A designer coat priced at $400.00 receives a 25% clearance markdown ($300.00 subtotal) and an additional 15% VIP member loyalty discount. The final price is $300 − (15% of $300) = $300 − $45 = <strong>$255.00</strong>, yielding a total saving of $145.00 (36.25% effective discount).
      </p>

      <!-- 8. COMMON MISTAKES -->
      <h2>Common Mistakes When Calculating Discounts</h2>

      <h3>Adding Multiple Discounts Together</h3>
      <p>
        Adding percentage discounts directly (e.g. 30% + 20% = 50%) overestimates your savings. Sequential markdowns apply only to the declining intermediate balance.
      </p>

      <h3>Calculating the Discount From the Wrong Price</h3>
      <p>
        Always clarify whether additional promo codes apply to the original list price or the current clearance sale price.
      </p>

      <h3>Forgetting Sales Tax</h3>
      <p>
        The final cash register amount paid includes state, county, and municipal sales taxes added to the discounted subtotal.
      </p>

      <h3>Confusing Discount Amount With Discount Percentage</h3>
      <p>
        A $30 markdown on a $500 item is a 6% discount, whereas a 30% discount on a $500 item is a $150 reduction.
      </p>

      <h3>Ignoring Rounding</h3>
      <p>
        Retail checkout systems round fractional cents at each item line, which can lead to minor penny differences compared to rounded estimates.
      </p>

      <!-- 9. IMPORTANT INFORMATION AND DISCLAIMER -->
      <h2>Important Information</h2>
      <p>
        Please keep the following principles in mind when reviewing your discount calculations:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Mathematical Simulation:</strong> Projections use exact commercial arithmetic and percentage compounding formulas.</li>
        <li><strong>Merchant Policy Variations:</strong> Retailers may apply coupons, store credits, gift cards, and promotional codes in differing sequences according to their specific terms of sale.</li>
        <li><strong>Tax & Legal Disclaimer:</strong> Tax treatments vary across jurisdictions. This tool provides estimates for shopping budgeting and educational reference.</li>
      </ul>

    </section>
  </Fragment>

